 ## Background
-Visibility culling is a fundamental acceleration technique in computer graphics, particularly in volume rendering where data density is high. The concept of frustum culling has been standard since the early days of 3D graphics. More specialized techniques, such as opacity map-based culling and view-dependent preprocessing, have been developed to handle the specific challenges of volumetric data, such as transparency and complex internal structures. The use of lookup tables for cell-based culling represents a more recent optimization for adaptive mesh refinement (AMR) data.
+The concept of visibility culling in volume rendering has evolved from simple frustum checks to complex, view-dependent, and feedback-driven mechanisms. Early implementations focused on reducing the overhead of per-pixel visibility tests by grouping pixels into screen regions [S1, unofficial]. As hardware capabilities increased, techniques like occlusion clipping using orthogonal opacity maps were introduced to handle depth-based occlusion more efficiently [S1, unofficial]. The rise of hierarchical data structures (octrees, AMR) enabled more sophisticated culling strategies, such as view-dependent visibility preprocessing and adaptive empty voxel skipping [S1, unofficial][S2]. Recent developments emphasize interactive performance, incorporating probabilistic culling and quality estimation to maintain frame rates in large-scale or VR contexts [S2][S1, unofficial].
 
